A||||10||Standard||225's and slushy snow don't mix...||||8||
Z||000000||Standard||11-16-2000||02:03 AM||||I knew there was a reason I wasn't gonna drive my GT in the winter.. I kept telling my self that I'll get the 84 up and running in time..  Now I'm just kicking myself..<P>We finally got our first real snow here in minnesota, and even though the highways weren't too slippery I was taking it easy. Everything was fine until I got to my off-ramp. I saw that it was pretty slushy, so I tried to slow down before I hit the slush. Didn't get quite slow enough, though, and as soon as I hit it I felt the rear end coming around. The next thing I remember is watching the diamond marker sign slam into my passenger side mirror, and hearing the crunch. After some (a lot) of screamed obscenities I get out to look at the damage.. passenger side ground f/x are pretty much gone, nice notch in the pass. side door panel. It looks like the post hit my door panel first, then took out the mirror. <P>It could have been worse, there were 2 other marker signs behind that one, and judging from the tire tracks I missed one by a few inches. <P>If I don't get the 84 driving I'm gonna have to put the 195's from the front of my car on the back, and use the steel 13 inchers on the front of the GT...  Might look a bit wierd, but it'll be a h*ll of a lot better than almost bald 225's on the back.  I can break them loose in a little bit of snow when I'm in 3rd gear going 20mph with just a little bit of throttle.. ugh  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">  Anybody giving out donations of body parts?  Christmas time is coming up....<BR><P>------------------<BR>Mike -<BR>Black 86.5 GT 5 Spd, 151,000 miles and counting!         <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/BURNOUT.gif">      <BR>Red 84 Sport Coupe - winter donut maker   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/asthanos.gif"> ||||reg||8
Z||000001||RossT||11-16-2000||02:11 AM||||That really bites the big one. We have 18 inches of snow already and its cold (even for us).  I will pay you to check out that 85gt for me, if the guy every gets around to sending me some pics.<P>sorry for your misfortunes.  At least you were not hurt.<p>[This message has been edited by RossT (edited 11-16-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Old Lar||11-16-2000||08:41 AM||||Sorry about your removal of road markers.  I learned along time ago (on a Vega GT wagon and wide oval tires) that fat tires and snow don't work.  Thats why my Fiero is parked until spring.  I drove it twice in the snow and it didn't like snow.||||reg||9
Z||000003||fldevel||11-16-2000||09:39 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Old Lar:<BR><B>(on a Vega GT wagon and wide oval tires) </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>oval tires ?? musta been a rough ride :P<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Uaana||11-16-2000||02:16 PM||||I know how you feel..<BR>Got a little jittery on my ride home last night from work slick as snot down in Eden Prarie where I work but up north by BP it was just wet..<BR>I feel another rant about idiots in sport ute's coming on though.. "I got 4WD and a cell phone planted in my ear! I can drive through anything at any speed and tailgate little cars all I want cus sport ute's are safe!"<P>I have to go lie down now.. the tremors are starting again..<BR>BTW whats wrong with your 84?||||reg||1
Z||000005||DrRodey||11-16-2000||02:31 PM||||I know all to well the horrors of Snow and larger tires.... 32x12.5R15s on a Wrangler with 3" of lift makes for quite the ride when we get snow...I've already put the stock Tires back on for the winter.  As for the Fieros?// They are nicely parked for the season..   <P>Sorry to hear about your streak of bad luck though.. if ya need any help with it look me up though..<P>Rodey<P>------------------<BR>'88 Formula CONVERTABLE<BR>'85 Coupe<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://freepages.ugo.com/RodeysPlace/files/fieroPage.JPG">     <BR> <A HREF="http://freepages.ugo.com/RodeysPlace/HomePlace.html" TARGET=_blank>freepages.ugo.com/RodeysPlace/HomePlace.html</A>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Jimmy||11-16-2000||03:09 PM||||Sorry to hear about the fiero Standard.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> I myself made a late night run to the NAPA in St. Louis Park at about 11:30 PM for flywheel bolts.  I was all over the road!  Spun out twice doing about 20 on side roads after getting off 100.  I guess I really needed those flywheel bolts.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000007||TheFiero64||11-16-2000||04:54 PM||||Sorry about your GT. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> I drove my Fiero half a winter and decided that I would never drive it in the snow again. Not because of the way the Fiero handled but because I was afraid of my babby getting hurt.<P>P.S. I hate road salt or getting stuck behind a Sander.<P>------------------<BR>88 Coupe V6<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/TheFiero64_signature1.gif">  <p>[This message has been edited by TheFiero64 (edited 11-16-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||Master_Sushi||11-16-2000||07:15 PM||||Ouch that sucks  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">. I too had a similar thing happen to me a while back in the middle of a wet, oily median. The biggest thing I also remember is the rear end of the car coming around. It seems like mid engine cars are harder to control in a bad skid then front engine (to me anyway). The main thing is no one got hurt. I would think smashing a few plastic body panels is easier to fix then screwing up your front suspension (which I did). But from what I read on here and from experiance fieros are not that hard to get fixed up again.||||reg||1
Z||000009||DaRkLoRD||11-17-2000||12:22 AM||||I'm planning to store my GT next winter.. and since I probably won't be able to afford a winter vehicle, I'll be back to hitching rides with friends etc...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>the following year, when I have a bit more money, I'm going to look into buying a used jeep grand cherokee or chevy blazer to drive in the winter.<P>------------------<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||3
Z||000010||Standard||11-17-2000||02:05 AM||||Thanks for the sympathy, guys  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  The ground f/x already had about a chunk missing on the rear, this just did away with most of it on the front  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif">  My pass. side mirror never worked anyways.. the biggest problem is the fender tore out of the wheel well, and now the door hing stuff is exposed to the road salt and slush..  <P>My 84 has some brake problems, mainly the pedal goes straight to the floor.  Replaced and bled the master cylinder, didn't change anything.  I would bleed the brakes but all the bleeders are either broken off or frozen solid.  And I just took the heater core out of it to use in the GT.  <P>Since I have no money right now, I swapped the 195's from the front of the GT to the back, and put the 185/80/13 steel rims on the front.. man does it look a bit ghetto.. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif">  gave me a whole lot more traction, though.  Hope I learned my lesson, too.  I'm gonna take it waaaay easy this winter..<P>------------------<BR>Mike -<BR>Black 86.5 GT 5 Spd, 153,000 miles and some busted up ground f/x  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/redface.gif"> <BR>Red 84 Sport Coupe - winter donut maker    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/asthanos.gif"> ||||reg||1
